{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfZfDqiRc5KX1o7kwgZh9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I wrote the candidate `individual_balance_ideal_alternation`, a refinement of the incumbent `ideal_alternation_with_balance`. It passes the self-check, but its PSIS-LOO came out unreliable on the short smoke fit, so it may not end up with a trusted fit.\n\n**The one change:** the incumbent gives everyone the same penalty for lopsided heads/tails counts. Here each person has their own penalty, drawn from a population distribution, built the same way the model already handles each person's ideal alternation rate. The personal ideal alternation rate is unchanged. This answers the only critique that survives the FDR correction, `participant_spread_balance_pref`: people differ in how much they prefer the more balanced sequence (observed SD 0.18 against the model's 0.143, q = 0.016).\n\n**Self-check result:** `OK`. The model loads, its log-probability is finite, it meets the data contract, and the smoke fit (100 draws, 1 chain) gave a finite ELPD-LOO of −987.2.\n\n**Two things the check didn't settle:**\n- **Unreliable PSIS-LOO:** 4.7% of trials had Pareto k above 0.5, against a 1% tolerance. A short single-chain fit may be partly to blame, but the new per-person weights, each estimated from about 64 trials, may also cause it. If the full fit is still unreliable, the model counts as untrusted.\n- **Not checked:** convergence (R-hat can't be computed from one chain) and novelty against the live models. The novelty gate should pass, since the predictions differ from the incumbent's for each participant.\n\nThe files are in `.../experiment1/model_loop/iter_2/candidate_3/`: `hypothesis.md`, `model_name.txt` and `candidate.py`."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2501,"cache_read_input_tokens":54256,"cache_creation":{"